Excerpts from netnews.rec.games.hack: 28-Nov-93 Re: Slow Hack by Roger
Christman:
> I also find the 286 640K very slow, which is the primary reason why
> I still play off of Unix.  Indeed, the Entire program is more than
> 1 MB, and can safely fit within 2 MB.  However, we certainly don't
> need to have the Entire program resident in memory.

No, and you don't; NetHack on a PC uses overlays.  Just moving around,
you (shouldn't) be doing disk access to swap in overlays.  The slowness
comes from the fact that NetHack 3.1 does a lot more work per turn than
the older versions because of things like the line-of-sight code, much
more complex potential monster behaviors, etc.

> I'd be happy to just have the basic stuff (player data, monster data,
> current level, object code for moving around, using common objects,
> and fighting.  I would think that much of this could safely fit
> even within 640K.
>  
> Things that could be kept on disk and not memory resident would include
> praying, sacrificing, invoking artifacts, creating dungeon levels.
> If it's something I don't do very often, I wouldn't mind waiting
> 3-5 seconds for it.  I could probably even live with a slowness when
> I zap wands and drink from fountains.

This has already been done (although probably not as you've described). 
Coding overlays is sort of like trying to optimize for locality of
virtual memory access considering frequency of use.  It's not anywhere
close to being as easy as you make it sound.  Try it yourself; get the
source and try to improve things.

> I have heard of an overlay thing.  If it were better constructed,
> maybe we wouldn't be noticing as much slowness on our 286's.
> Unfortunately, apparently we 286 users are viewed to be in the
> minority and aren't high up in the priority list.  Too bad that
> not all of us can go out and by an upgrade.  Also too bad that
> not all of us can play on a Unix platform as an alternative.

I hate to tell you this, but there is absolutely nothing the dev team
can do about your machine, no matter how well they program.  If you want
better performance, upgrade your machine or use an older version of
NetHack (like 3.0j).  It is neither possible nor reasonable to get the
same performance on a fast unix box and a vintage PC AT (or clone).

Your complaint makes me think of an Apple ][ or a C64 owner: "I want to
play NetHack 3.1 on my 10 year old 64K machine with a 1MHz CPU!"  Do you
expect to be able to run Windows NT on your machine?  At some point
you've got to draw the line.

At the least, get another 1 or 2MB of RAM so that NetHack can get the
overlays from RAM disk rather than your hard drive.  You'll probably
notice that everything else on you system will work better too, but
rememeber what's important.... :-)

